%======================================================================
\section{The everyday observation}

Move a bar magnet into a coil connected to a galvanometer: the needle kicks.
Hold the magnet still inside the coil: the needle returns to zero.
Pull the magnet out: the needle kicks the other way.
Spin a bicycle dynamo: a lamp lights.

Nothing in the coil is ``touching'' the magnet in a mechanical sense, yet an
\textbf{electromotive force (emf)} appears. That phenomenon is
\textbf{electromagnetic induction}.

You already know two related facts from magnetism:
\begin{itemize}
  \item a current produces a magnetic field;
  \item a magnetic field exerts a force on a moving charge / current-carrying wire
        ($F=qvB$ or $F=BIL$).
\end{itemize}
Induction is the reverse story: \textbf{changing magnetism can drive charges
around a circuit}, producing an emf (and a current if the circuit is closed).

\begin{mdframed}[style=misbox]
\noindent\textbf{Common mix-up.}
A \emph{steady} magnetic field through a coil does \emph{not} induce an emf.
Induction cares about \textbf{change} (or relative motion that changes the
flux), not about ``having a field nearby''. A magnet sitting quietly inside a
coil produces zero reading on the galvanometer.
\end{mdframed}

%======================================================================
\section{Magnetic flux}

\subsection{What flux measures}
Think of magnetic field lines as rain falling through a window.
\textbf{Magnetic flux} $\Phi$ measures how many field lines pass through an area
--- it is the bookkeeping quantity Faraday's law needs.

For a uniform field $B$ through a flat area $A$,
\[
\Phi = B A \cos\theta = B_\perp A,
\]
where $\theta$ is the angle between $\vec B$ and the \textbf{normal} to the area
(the direction ``straight out'' of the face of the loop).

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05]
  \draw[thick,fill=blue!8] (0,0) -- (2.4,0.4) -- (2.4,2.0) -- (0,1.6) -- cycle;
  \node[font=\small] at (1.2,1.0) {$A$};
  \draw[-{Latex},very thick,blue!50!black] (1.2,1.0) -- (1.2,2.9)
    node[above,font=\small] {normal};
  \draw[-{Latex},very thick,posC] (1.2,1.0) -- (2.6,2.6)
    node[above right,font=\small] {$\vec B$};
  \draw[dashed] (1.2,1.0) -- (2.6,1.0);
  \draw (1.7,1.0) arc (0:45:0.5);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.95,1.25) {$\theta$};
  \node[below,align=center,font=\small] at (1.2,-0.55)
    {$\Phi = BA\cos\theta$\\[0.2em]
     max when $\theta=0$ (field $\perp$ area); zero when $\theta=90^\circ$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Unit of $\Phi$: \textbf{weber} (Wb). $1\,\mathrm{Wb}=1\,\mathrm{T\,m^{2}}$.
  \item For a coil of $N$ turns, the \textbf{flux linkage} is $N\Phi$.
        Faraday responds to the rate of change of flux linkage, not of $\Phi$ alone.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Three ways flux can change}
Since $\Phi = BA\cos\theta$, any of these can induce an emf:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Change $B$ (switch a nearby electromagnet on/off; move a magnet closer).
  \item Change $A$ (stretch a loop; slide a rod so the enclosed area grows).
  \item Change $\theta$ (rotate a coil in a magnetic field --- the generator idea).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Sign of $\Phi$.}
Choose a positive normal once and stick to it. If $\vec B$ reverses relative to
that normal, $\Phi$ changes sign. A $180^\circ$ flip of a coil in a fixed field
gives $\Delta\Phi = 2BA$ in magnitude --- a favourite exam trick.
\end{mdframed}

%======================================================================
\section{Faraday's law --- magnitude}

The magnitude of the average induced emf in a coil of $N$ turns is
\[
\mathcal{E} = N\,\frac{|\Delta\Phi|}{\Delta t}.
\]
In words: \textbf{emf $\propto$ how fast the flux linkage changes}.

A compact form that also encodes direction (see Lenz) is
\[
\mathcal{E} = -N\,\frac{\mathrm{d}\Phi}{\mathrm{d}t}.
\]
The minus sign is Lenz's law written into the formula.

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Exam habits.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Broken circuit $\Rightarrow$ induced \textbf{emf} still exists; no sustained
        induced \textbf{current}.
  \item Closed circuit $\Rightarrow$ induced emf \emph{and} induced current
        $I=\mathcal{E}/R$.
  \item Soft-iron core: gathers and guides field lines $\Rightarrow$ larger
        $|\Delta\Phi|$ for the same magnet motion $\Rightarrow$ larger emf.
  \item Faster change (smaller $\Delta t$ for the same $|\Delta\Phi|$) $\Rightarrow$
        larger $|\mathcal{E}|$.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

\subsection{Worked numerical example --- loop entering a field}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05]
  % field region
  \fill[gray!12] (2.0,-0.9) rectangle (5.8,1.5);
  \foreach \x in {2.2,2.6,...,5.6} {
    \foreach \y in {-0.7,-0.3,...,1.3} {
      \fill[gray] (\x,\y) circle (1.0pt);
    }
  }
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (5.3,1.7) {$B$ out};
  % loop entering
  \draw[very thick] (0.3,-0.5) rectangle (2.8,1.1);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.55,0.3) {loop};
  \draw[-{Latex},very thick,posC] (1.55,-0.75) -- (2.6,-0.75)
    node[midway,below,font=\small] {$v$};
  \draw[<->] (2.0,1.65) -- (2.8,1.65);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (2.4,1.65) {entering};
  \node[below,align=center,font=\small] at (3.0,-1.35)
    {While entering: enclosed area grows $\Rightarrow$ $\Phi$ increases\\
     $\Rightarrow$ induced emf. Once wholly inside at constant $v$: $\Phi$ constant.};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\noindent\textbf{Example.}
A square conducting loop of side $\ell=6.0\,\mathrm{cm}$ and $N=1$ turn moves at
constant speed $v=5.0\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ into a uniform field
$B=0.80\,\mathrm{T}$ perpendicular to the plane of the loop.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
  \item Find the average induced emf while the loop is entering the field.
  \item How long does the entry take?
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Solution.}
During entry the flux rises from $0$ to $B\ell^{2}$:
\[
|\Delta\Phi| = B\ell^{2} = (0.80)(0.060)^{2} = 2.88\times 10^{-3}\,\mathrm{Wb}.
\]
The time to travel one side-length is
\[
\Delta t = \frac{\ell}{v} = \frac{0.060}{5.0} = 0.012\,\mathrm{s}.
\]
Faraday:
\[
\mathcal{E} = N\frac{|\Delta\Phi|}{\Delta t}
= \frac{2.88\times 10^{-3}}{0.012} = 0.24\,\mathrm{V}.
\]
(Equivalently, for a single turn entering at constant $v$:
$\mathcal{E}=B\ell v=(0.80)(0.060)(5.0)=0.24\,\mathrm{V}$.)

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{What-if.}
If the number of turns becomes $N=50$ (same $\ell$, $B$, $v$), the emf scales by
$N$: $\mathcal{E}=50\times 0.24=12\,\mathrm{V}$.
If the loop is already wholly inside and still moving at constant $v$,
$\Delta\Phi=0$ $\Rightarrow$ $\mathcal{E}=0$ (emfs on opposite sides cancel).
\end{mdframed}

%======================================================================
\section{Lenz's law --- direction}

\textbf{Lenz's law:} the induced current always flows so that its magnetic effect
\textbf{opposes the change} of flux that produced it.

This is energy conservation in disguise. If the induced current \emph{helped}
the change, you could get free energy from a magnet and a coil. Nature forbids
that: the induced effect fights you.

\subsection{Magnet approaching a coil}
Suppose the N-pole of a bar magnet approaches the left face of a closed coil.
The rightward field through the coil is \textbf{increasing}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05]
  % magnet
  \draw[thick,fill=posC!20] (-3.2,-0.35) rectangle (-1.4,0.55);
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (-2.7,0.1) {S};
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (-1.85,0.1) {N};
  \draw[-{Latex},very thick] (-1.25,0.1) -- (-0.55,0.1)
    node[midway,above,font=\scriptsize] {approach};
  % coil
  \draw[thick] (0.3,-1.0) ellipse (0.45 and 1.15);
  \draw[thick] (0.3,0.15) arc (90:270:0.28 and 1.15);
  \foreach \y in {-0.7,-0.35,0,0.35,0.7} {
    \draw[thick] (0.55,\y) -- (1.85,\y);
  }
  \draw[thick] (1.85,-0.7) arc (-90:90:0.28 and 0.7);
  % induced field
  \draw[-{Latex},very thick,negC] (0.3,0.1) -- (-0.35,0.1);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,negC,align=center] at (-0.15,0.55)
    {induced\\$B$ left};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,align=center] at (0.3,-1.55)
    {near face acts as\\[-0.1em]\textbf{N-pole}};
  \node[below,align=center,font=\small] at (0,-2.2)
    {Increasing flux to the right $\Rightarrow$ induced current makes a field to the
     \textbf{left}\\
     (near face becomes N) $\Rightarrow$ magnet is \textbf{repelled}. You must push.};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\subsection{Magnet leaving a coil}
If the same N-pole is pulled \textbf{away}, the rightward flux is
\textbf{decreasing}. The induced current now tries to \textbf{maintain} that flux:
near face becomes an S-pole and \textbf{attracts} the departing magnet. You must
still do work to pull it away.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95]
