\paragraph{Practical recipe.}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Decide whether flux through the coil is \emph{increasing} or \emph{decreasing},
        and in which direction the field points.
  \item Induced current creates a field that fights that change
        (oppose increase; try to maintain a decrease).
  \item Use the right-hand grip rule on the coil to find the current sense.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{mdframed}[style=misbox]
\noindent\textbf{Trap.}
Oppose the \emph{change}, not the field itself. If flux is decreasing, the
induced current tries to \textbf{keep} the flux (same direction as the original
field) --- it does \emph{not} reverse the original field on purpose; it only
fills in what is being lost.
\end{mdframed}

%======================================================================
\section{Motional emf --- from first principles}

\subsection{Why moving charges in $B$ feel a force}
A free charge $q$ moving with velocity $\vec v$ in a magnetic field feels
\[
\vec F = q\,\vec v\times\vec B
\]
(magnitude $F=qvB\sin\theta$). This is the same rule that gives $F=BIL$ for a
current-carrying wire: a current is just many charges drifting.

Now imagine a conducting rod of length $\ell$ sliding sideways through a uniform
field (the ``cutting field lines'' picture). Every free charge inside the metal
is being carried along with the rod at speed $v$, so each feels a magnetic force
along the rod.

\subsection{Separation of $+$ and $-$ --- the origin of the emf}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
  \foreach \x in {0,0.4,...,4.0} {
    \foreach \y in {0,0.4,...,2.0} {
      \fill[gray] (\x,\y) circle (1.0pt);
    }
  }
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (4.55,1.0) {$B$ out};
  \draw[very thick] (0.5,1.0) -- (3.5,1.0);
  \node[left,font=\small] at (0.5,1.0) {$P$};
  \node[right,font=\small] at (3.5,1.0) {$Q$};
  \draw[-{Latex},very thick,posC] (2.0,1.0) -- (2.0,0.15)
    node[below,font=\small] {$v$};
  % charges
  \node[negC,font=\small\bfseries] at (0.85,1.35) {$-$};
  \node[posC,font=\small\bfseries] at (3.15,1.35) {$+$};
  \draw[-{Latex},negC] (1.4,1.0) -- (0.9,1.0);
  \draw[-{Latex},posC] (2.6,1.0) -- (3.1,1.0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,align=center] at (2.0,2.45)
    {Magnetic force drives $+q$ toward $Q$ (Fleming RH)\\
     $\Rightarrow$ $Q$ becomes positive, $P$ negative $\Rightarrow$ p.d.\ across rod};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

As charges pile up, an electric field builds inside the rod from $Q$ toward $P$.
The electric force $qE$ on each free charge grows until it balances the magnetic
force:
\[
qE = qvB
\qquad\Rightarrow\qquad
E = vB
\qquad\Rightarrow\qquad
\mathcal{E} = E\ell = B\ell v
\]
(for $v$, $\ell$, and $B$ mutually perpendicular). More generally,
\[
\mathcal{E} = (v B\sin\theta)\,\ell,
\]
where $\theta$ is the angle between $\vec v$ and $\vec B$.

\paragraph{Direction.}
Use \textbf{Fleming's right-hand rule} (generator rule):
thumb = motion, first finger = field, second finger = force on $+q$ /
sense of induced current if the circuit is closed.

\subsection{Open rod vs closed circuit}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Open rod:} charges separate until equilibrium; a p.d.\ appears,
        but \textbf{no sustained current} flows. No magnetic drag force on the rod
        (drag requires a current).
  \item \textbf{Rod on conducting rails} closing a loop: current flows;
        $F=BIL$ on the rod \textbf{opposes} the motion (Lenz). To keep $v$
        constant you must apply an equal external force; the mechanical work you
        do becomes electrical energy in the resistor
        ($P=\mathcal{E}I=B^{2}\ell^{2}v^{2}/R$).
\end{itemize}

\begin{mdframed}[style=misbox]
\noindent\textbf{Trap.}
``There is an induced emf, so there must be an induced current.''
False. Emf is a cause; current also needs a closed path.
HKDSE loves open rods falling in the Earth's field.
\end{mdframed}

\worked{2012}{32}{A}{47}{../2012_q32.png}{%
The falling copper rod cuts the Earth's field ($B$ out of the page), so free
charges in the rod experience $F=qvB$ and a motional emf appears across $PQ$:
statement~(1) is true ($\mathcal{E}=B\ell v$).
There is \textbf{no closed circuit}, so no sustained induced current~(2) is
false. With no current there is no magnetic drag force $BIL$, so the rod still
falls with acceleration $g$~(3) is false.
Hence only~(1) $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{A}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Eddy currents}

\subsection{What they are}
In a \textbf{solid} piece of metal moving through a field (or sitting in a
changing field), you can imagine many little closed conducting loops inside the
bulk. Faraday + Lenz then drive circulating currents --- \textbf{eddy currents}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  \fill[gray!15] (0,0) rectangle (3.2,2.0);
  \draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (3.2,2.0);
  \draw[-{Latex},thick,posC] (0.6,1.0) arc (180:-160:0.55);
  \draw[-{Latex},thick,posC] (2.2,1.0) arc (180:-160:0.55);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.6,-0.35) {eddy loops in a solid plate};
  \begin{scope}[xshift=4.2cm]
    \fill[gray!15] (0,0) rectangle (3.2,2.0);
    \draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (3.2,2.0);
    \foreach \x in {0.8,1.6,2.4} {
      \draw[thick] (\x,0) -- (\x,2.0);
    }
    \node[font=\scriptsize,align=center] at (1.6,-0.45)
      {slotted plate: eddy paths broken\\$\Rightarrow$ weaker currents};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\subsection{Useful applications}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Magnetic braking} (some trains, roller-coasters, sensitive
        balances): motion through $B$ induces eddy currents; Lenz produces a
        drag force opposing the motion --- smooth braking without contact pads.
  \item \textbf{Induction heating} / cooktops: a changing field induces eddy
        currents in a metal pan; $I^{2}R$ heating cooks the food (the pan gets
        hot; the cooker surface itself need not).
  \item \textbf{Damping} of oscillations in galvanometer coils / metal frames.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Hazards and how to reduce them}
Eddy currents in a transformer's iron core would waste energy as heat and reduce
efficiency. Remedy: use a \textbf{laminated} core (thin sheets insulated from
each other) so eddy loops cannot grow large. Slotting a braking plate similarly
cuts eddy paths when you want \emph{less} braking.

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Link to Lenz.}
Eddy braking is Lenz's law in a solid: induced currents create a magnetic force
that opposes the relative motion causing the flux change. Pull a copper sheet
out of a strong magnet gap and you feel the drag.
\end{mdframed}

%======================================================================
\section{Simple generators}

\subsection{Rotating coil}
A coil of $N$ turns, area $A$, rotating at angular speed $\omega$ in uniform $B$
has flux $\Phi=BA\cos\omega t$ (suitable zero of $t$), so
\[
\mathcal{E}=NBA\omega\sin\omega t,
\qquad
\mathcal{E}_0 = NBA\omega.
\]
The emf generated in the coil is inherently \textbf{alternating}. Whether the
\emph{load} sees a.c.\ or unsteady d.c.\ depends on the contacts (slip rings vs
commutator).

\subsection{AC output --- slip rings}
Two continuous slip rings + brushes keep each end of the coil connected to the
same terminal of the external circuit. Output to the load is ac.

\subsection{DC output --- commutator (split rings)}
A split-ring commutator reverses the coil-to-load connections every half turn,
so the current in the \textbf{load} stays in one direction (unsteady / pulsating
d.c.). Inside the coil the current still reverses naturally with rotation.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
  \draw[thick,fill=gray!20] (-2.2,-0.8) rectangle (-1.4,0.8);
  \node[font=\bfseries] at (-1.8,0) {S};
  \draw[thick,fill=gray!20] (1.4,-0.8) rectangle (2.2,0.8);
  \node[font=\bfseries] at (1.8,0) {N};
  \draw[thick] (-0.9,-0.55) rectangle (0.9,0.55);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {coil};
  \draw[-{Latex}] (0.5,0.75) arc (40:-200:0.55);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.15,1.05) {rotate};
  \draw[thick] (0,-0.55) -- (0,-1.35);
  \draw[thick] (-0.25,-1.55) arc (180:0:0.25);
  \draw[thick] (0.25,-1.55) arc (0:-180:0.25);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,align=center] at (0,-2.05) {commutator\\(split rings)};
  \draw[thick] (-0.55,-1.55) -- (-0.55,-2.55) -- (0.55,-2.55) -- (0.55,-1.55);
  \draw[thick] (-0.25,-2.55) -- (-0.25,-3.0);
  \draw[thick] (0.25,-2.55) -- (0.25,-3.0);
  \draw[thick] (-0.55,-3.15) rectangle (0.55,-3.45);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (0,-3.3) {load};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\paragraph{Factors that increase $|\mathcal{E}_0|$.}
Stronger $B$, more turns $N$, larger area $A$, higher rotation rate $\omega$,
soft-iron core (boosts effective $B$).

\worked{2022}{28}{D}{}{../2022_q28.png}{%
At the instant shown the coil is being driven clockwise: side $AB$ moves
downward. Induced current (Fleming RH) experiences a magnetic force that
\textbf{opposes} the motion, so the force on $AB$ is upward~(1) true
(Lenz / energy conservation).
The commutator reverses the \emph{connections to the load} each half-turn; it
does \textbf{not} ``reverse the current in the coil'' as statement~(2) claims
--- the coil current reverses because of rotation itself~(2) false.
With a split-ring commutator the load current is unidirectional but not steady
(unsteady d.c.)~(3) true.
Hence (1) and (3) $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{D}.}

%======================================================================
\section{QB examples (QB\_407)}

\qbtext{PHY14071101}{%
A coil of metal wire is placed perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field.
The coil is then rotated through $180^\circ$.
The magnitude of the average induced e.m.f.\ is independent of
\begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item the area of the coil;
  \item the flux density of the magnetic field;
  \item the number of turns of the coil;
  \item the resistance of the coil.
\end{enumerate}
}{D}{%
Initial flux $\Phi_i = BA$ (taking $\theta=0$). After a $180^\circ$ flip,
$\Phi_f = -BA$, so $|\Delta\Phi|=2BA$.
Average emf $\mathcal{E}=N|\Delta\Phi|/\Delta t=N(2BA)/\Delta t$.
This depends on $N$, $B$, $A$, and time, but \textbf{not} on resistance.
Resistance affects induced \emph{current} $I=\mathcal{E}/R$, not the emf itself
$\Rightarrow$ \textbf{D}.}

\qbtext{PHY14071104}{%
A bar magnet moves away from a coil connected to a lamp.
Which of the following will increase the power dissipated by the lamp?
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*),leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item Insert a soft-iron core into the coil.
  \item Increase the speed of the magnet.
  \item Replace the lamp with one having a lower resistance.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item (1) only
  \item (1) and (2) only
  \item (2) and (3) only
  \item (1), (2) and (3)
\end{enumerate}
}{D}{%
Power in the lamp $P=\mathcal{E}^{2}/R$ (for a given emf).
(1)~Soft-iron core increases flux linkage change $\Rightarrow$ larger
$|\mathcal{E}|$.
(2)~Faster motion $\Rightarrow$ larger $|\Delta\Phi/\Delta t|$ $\Rightarrow$ larger
$|\mathcal{E}|$.
(3)~Smaller $R$ $\Rightarrow$ larger $P=\mathcal{E}^{2}/R$.
All three help $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{D}.}

\qbtext{PHY14071106}{%
When a metal rod $PQ$ moves with constant velocity across a uniform magnetic
field, an e.m.f.\ is induced across the rod.
Which statement is correct?
\begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item The magnitude of the e.m.f.\ depends on the speed of the rod.
  \item $P$ is at a higher potential than $Q$.
  \item A magnetic force is acting on the rod to oppose its motion.
  \item A direct current is induced.
\end{enumerate}
}{A}{%
$\mathcal{E}=B\ell v$ (perpendicular case), so the emf depends on speed~(A).
Potential which end is higher depends on the directions of $\vec v$ and
$\vec B$ --- not fixed by the stem alone~(B) unreliable.
With an open rod there is no sustained current, so no magnetic drag $BIL$~(C)
false, and no direct current~(D) false.
$\Rightarrow$ \textbf{A}.}

\qbtext{PHY14071105}{%
The flux linkage through a plane coil placed in a uniform magnetic field depends on
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*),leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item the resistance of the coil;
  \item the number of turns in the coil;
  \item the angle between the normal to the coil and the direction of the magnetic field.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item (1) only
  \item (2) only
  \item (1) and (2) only
  \item (2) and (3) only
\end{enumerate}
}{D}{%
Flux linkage $=N\Phi=NBA\cos\theta$. It depends on $N$ and on orientation
$\theta$, but not on resistance $\Rightarrow$ (2) and (3) $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{D}.}

\qbtext{PHY14071112}{%
A bar magnet is held close to a coil connected to a galvanometer.
In which case(s) will the galvanometer detect a current?
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*),leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item Move both the magnet and the coil to the right at the same speed.
  \item Move the magnet to the right and the coil to the left.
  \item Spin the magnet.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item (1) only
  \item (3) only
  \item (1) and (2) only
  \item (2) and (3) only
\end{enumerate}
}{D}{%
Induction needs \textbf{relative} motion that changes flux.
(1)~Same velocity $\Rightarrow$ no relative motion $\Rightarrow$ no change of flux
through the coil.
(2)~Relative approach/separation $\Rightarrow$ changing flux.
(3)~Spinning changes the field pattern through the coil $\Rightarrow$ changing
flux.
Hence (2) and (3) $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{D}.}

\qbtext{PHY14071115}{%
A circular coil of $N$ turns and radius $r$ is placed normally to a uniform
field $B$. It is then rotated through $180^\circ$ in time $t$.
The magnitude of the average induced e.m.f.\ is
\begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item $NB\pi r^{2}/t$
  \item $2NB\pi r^{2}/t$
  \item $NB\pi r^{2}/(2t)$
  \item $NBr^{2}/t$
\end{enumerate}
}{B}{%
$A=\pi r^{2}$. Initial $\Phi=BA$; after $180^\circ$, $\Phi=-BA$;
$|\Delta\Phi|=2BA=2B\pi r^{2}$.
\[
\mathcal{E}=N\frac{|\Delta\Phi|}{t}=\frac{2NB\pi r^{2}}{t}
\Rightarrow\textbf{B}.
\]}

%======================================================================
\section{Formula summary}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Idea & Formula / rule \\
\midrule
Magnetic flux & $\Phi=BA\cos\theta$ \\
Flux linkage & $N\Phi$ \\
Faraday (magnitude) & $\mathcal{E}=N|\Delta\Phi|/\Delta t$ \\
Lenz & induced effects oppose the flux \emph{change} \\
Force on moving charge & $F=qvB\sin\theta$ \\
Motional emf & $\mathcal{E}=B\ell v$ (perpendicular case) \\
Loop entering field & $\mathcal{E}=NB\ell v$ (single face cutting) \\
AC generator peak & $\mathcal{E}_0=NBA\omega$ \\
Induced current & $I=\mathcal{E}/R$ (closed circuit only) \\
Electrical power from drag & $P=\mathcal{E}I=B^{2}\ell^{2}v^{2}/R$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Before the exam --- checklist.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em,itemsep=0.15em]
  \item Is flux actually \emph{changing}? Steady $B$ through a fixed coil $\Rightarrow$ no emf.
  \item Emf vs current: is the circuit closed?
  \item Lenz: oppose the \emph{change}, not blindly the field.
  \item Motional emf: open rod can have voltage with zero current and zero magnetic drag.
  \item Solid metal $\Rightarrow$ think eddy currents (braking / heating / laminations).
  \item Generator: slip rings $\to$ ac load; commutator $\to$ unsteady d.c.\ load.
  \item $180^\circ$ rotation: $|\Delta\Phi|=2BA$, not $BA$.
  \item Soft-iron core increases flux change; resistance does not change the emf.
\end{enumerate}
\end{mdframed}

\clearpage
\section{Practice questions}

Attempt these closed-book. Answers are at the end.

\begin{mdframed}[style=pracbox]
\noindent\textbf{Practice --- conceptual}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A coil sits in a uniform constant $B$-field. Does a steady current appear
        in the coil? Explain.
  \item Why does pulling a magnet out of a closed coil require more force than
        inserting it into an open-circuited coil (same speed)?
  \item A metal frame enters a uniform $B$-field at constant speed $v$. Why is
        there induced emf while entering, but none when the frame is wholly
        inside and still moving?
  \item Why does slotting a copper plate reduce magnetic braking when the plate
        enters a strong magnetic field?
\end{enumerate}
\end{mdframed}

\begin{mdframed}[style=pracbox]
\noindent\textbf{Practice --- calculation}
A square loop of side $\ell=0.10\,\mathrm{m}$ and $N=50$ turns moves at
$v=2.0\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ into a uniform field $B=0.40\,\mathrm{T}$ perpendicular
to the plane of the loop.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Find the magnitude of the induced emf while the loop is entering the field.
  \item If the loop resistance is $R=5.0\,\Omega$, find the induced current during
        entry, and the magnetic drag force on the leading side.
\end{enumerate}
\end{mdframed}

\subsection*{Practice answers}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item No. $\Delta\Phi=0$ for constant $B$ and fixed orientation/area, so
        $\mathcal{E}=0$.
  \item Closed coil: induced current $\Rightarrow$ opposing magnetic force (Lenz).
        Open coil: emf but no current $\Rightarrow$ no magnetic drag.
  \item Entering: enclosed flux increases ($\Delta A$). Fully inside at constant
        $v$: $\Phi=BA$ constant $\Rightarrow$ no net emf (emfs on opposite sides cancel).
  \item Slots break eddy-current paths $\Rightarrow$ smaller eddy $I$ $\Rightarrow$
        weaker opposing force.
  \item (a)~$\mathcal{E}=NB\ell v=(50)(0.40)(0.10)(2.0)=4.0\,\mathrm{V}$.
        (b)~$I=\mathcal{E}/R=4.0/5.0=0.80\,\mathrm{A}$;
        drag on leading side $F=BIL=(0.40)(0.80)(0.10)=0.032\,\mathrm{N}$
        (opposes motion).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Classification note.}
In this topic folder, \texttt{2014\_q17}, \texttt{2014\_q18}, \texttt{2016\_q21},
and \texttt{2024\_q17} are ultrasound / sound-interference questions and are
omitted. \texttt{2014\_q28} is about $B=\mu_0 (N/L)I$ for a solenoid carrying
\textbf{constant} d.c.\ (magnetism, no induction) and is also omitted.
Real induction DSE items available and used: \texttt{2012\_q32}, \texttt{2022\_q28}.
\end{mdframed}

