\qbtext{PHY14071102}{%
A rectangular metal plate parallel to the vertical is released from rest and
cuts across a uniform magnetic field throughout its motion. Neglect air
resistance. Which statement is correct?
\begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item A current is induced throughout the motion.
  \item A voltage is induced across sides $WX$ and $ZY$.
  \item The plate falls with an acceleration less than $g$.
  \item The plate falls with a constant speed.
\end{enumerate}
}{C}{%
A continuous metal plate supports \textbf{eddy-current} loops while it cuts the
field. Those currents experience a magnetic drag (Lenz) opposing the fall, so
the net downward acceleration is less than $g$~(C).
It does not instantly become constant speed~(D).
Whether a macroscopic ``current throughout'' or a voltage across particular
labelled sides is present depends on path geometry; the robust, syllabus-standard
conclusion is magnetic braking $\Rightarrow$ $a<g$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{C}.}

\qbtext{PHY14071109}{%
An aircraft of wing span $50\,\mathrm{m}$ flies at $200\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$.
The Earth's magnetic field is $1.0\times 10^{-5}\,\mathrm{T}$ and makes an angle
of $60^\circ$ with the travelling direction. Estimate the p.d.\ induced across
the wing tips.
\begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item $0.00173\,\mathrm{V}$
  \item $0.05\,\mathrm{V}$
  \item $0.0866\,\mathrm{V}$
  \item $0.1\,\mathrm{V}$
\end{enumerate}
}{C}{%
Motional emf $\mathcal{E}=B\ell v\sin\theta$ with $\theta=60^\circ$ between
$\vec v$ and $\vec B$:
\[
\mathcal{E}=(1.0\times 10^{-5})(50)(200)\sin 60^\circ
=0.10\times\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}=0.0866\,\mathrm{V}
\Rightarrow\textbf{C}.
\]
(Same physics as a rod cutting field lines --- the wings are the rod.)}

