input.new_string:
\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Link to Lenz.}
Eddy braking is Lenz's law in a solid: induced currents create a magnetic force
that opposes the relative motion causing the flux change. Pull a copper sheet
out of a strong magnet gap and you feel the drag.
\end{mdframed}

\subsection{Applications snapshot (induction everywhere)}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Bicycle dynamo / generator:} rotating coil (or rotating magnet)
        changes flux $\Rightarrow$ lighting emf.
  \item \textbf{Moving-coil microphone:} sound moves a coil in a magnetic field
        $\Rightarrow$ changing flux linkage $\Rightarrow$ audio signal emf
        (loudspeakers are the reverse: current $\to$ force).
  \item \textbf{Magnetic tape playback head:} magnetised tape varies $B$ at the
        head gap $\Rightarrow$ induced signal in the coil.
  \item \textbf{Transformer:} a.c.\ in the primary $\Rightarrow$ changing core flux
        $\Rightarrow$ secondary emf (see the AC \& transformers notes).
\end{itemize}

